  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\n30-40kt low level jet with a weak wave aloft continues to progress \nacross eastern South Dakota, responsible for storms in north central \nSouth Dakota at midnight. NAM BUFKIT profiles indicate about 3.5k \nj/kg CAPE in strong unidirectional flow of 30 to 50kts from about \n2km through 8km depth. Thus, expect splitting supercell storms with \na large hail threat. \n\nFor today then, surface high pressure to the north will result in a \nnorth breeze with drier air and slightly cooler temperatures. \nMixed winds in BUFKIT are on average about 15 to 20kts with an \neast to northeast trajectory.\n\nAs the high continues east, low level flow around this feature \nbecomes enhanced tonight, with 1/2km winds increasing to 25-30kts \nacross mostly western/central South Dakota. Warm advection at 700mb \nas well for the same area. NAM shows a weak wave early this\nmorning, with a northwest flow shortwave also clipping the \nnortheast early Monday. There is a little bit of a disconnect \nbetween each of these elements providing for some uncertainty into\nthe coverage and severity of storms. While not a universal \noutcome between CAMS, many are showing storms this evening out \nwest river weakening before low level jet re-invigoration over \ncentral South Dakota, as well as some storms dropping into the \nnortheast early Monday morning. \n\nLooks like this pattern, with nocturnal storms, continues into \nMonday night/Tuesday morning as well.\n\nIn regards to smoke, nothing new or earth shattering, just continued \nelevated smoke with again a unfavorable fetch for mixing down or \ntrapping smoke at the surface. \n\nAs for temperatures for the latter half of the work week, while we \nare still under a northwest flow regime, there is strengthening of \nan eastern CONUS upper trough, allowing for more direct flow from \nCanada and thus cooler temperatures, though it should be noted that \nthe latest NBM has shifted to a less drastic cool down (raised \nforecast temperatures about 2-3 degrees) Thursday/Friday/Saturday.",
